  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\nPeriods of showers lingering offshore will continue to slowly move \ninland through the early afternoon, with increased coverage of \nscattered showers later this afternoon into tonight. Light to \nmoderate rain is expected with rainfall rates peaking at 0.30-0.40 \ninches per hour. Overall, rainfall totals will be 0.05-0.20 inches \nfor Orange County and the Inland Empire; 0.10-0.50 inches for San \nDiego coast and valleys; 0.50-1 inch for the mountains; less than \n0.20 inch in the High Desert; and little to no rain is expected in \nthe lower deserts. Additionally, there is a chance of thunderstorms \n(10%) over the coastal waters and locally along the coast this \nafternoon through 10 PM. Where thunderstorms develop, expected \nerratic, gusty winds and localized flooding. Showers will taper off \nfrom northwest to southeast Tuesday morning. Snow levels are \nexpected to stay above 8000 ft during much of the precipitation \nleading to 2-4 inches of snow above 8000 ft. Snow levels will drop \nto 5500-6000 ft as precipitation tapers off, with only a dusting \npossible below 8000 ft. Winds will increase over the mountains and \ninto the deserts this evening into Tuesday morning. Peak gusts of 40 \nto 50 mph expected. \n\nMostly dry conditions expected Tuesday and Wednesday, with below \naverage temperatures. High temperatures will range from 2-12 degrees \nbelow seasonal averages. Wednesday will be a few degrees warmer than \nTuesday, but the \"warming trend\" will be brief with Thursday \nexpected to see widespread cooler conditions. For Thursday through \nSaturday highs will be 7-12 degrees below average for inland \nlocations. \n\nIn addition to cooler conditions on Thursday, another round of \nprecipitation and gusty winds can be expected. The system arriving \nlate Wednesday night/early Thursday will be driven by a stronger jet \nand will have enough IVT associated with it to be considered a weak \nAtmospheric River. While there still remains some uncertainty in the \nsystem's track, we are expecting more rain, stronger winds, and snow \nat lower elevations compared to tonight/Tuesday's system. NBM \nchances of exceeding 1 inch of rain from early Thursday to early \nFriday are 70-90 percent for areas north of San Diego County \n(highest chances in the San Bernardino mountains), 20-50 percent for \nSan Diego County, and less than 15 percent for the deserts. The \ninfluence from the jet stream will help increase instability, \nallowing for chances of isolated thunderstorms across the coastal \nbasin Thursday afternoon. This is expected to be a colder system \nwith snow levels expected to start out around 7500 ft Wednesday \nnight and fall to 6500 ft by Thursday evening. Some accumulating \nsnow looks possible around 6500 ft, with the majority of the \naccumulations above 7000 ft. The last hazard to mention for the \nWednesday night into Thursday time frame is the increase in westerly \nwinds over the mountains and deserts. First look at the wind \nforecast indicates peak gusts 40 to 55 mph, locally up to 60 mph \nthrough wind prone passes and canyons. It looks like precipitation \nand gusty winds will taper off Thursday into Friday morning with \ndry conditions expected Friday afternoon through Saturday. \n\nAnother system appears on the horizon, arriving Sunday and/or \nMonday. This one looks weaker, so not as wet or windy as the \nThursday system. Its projected location, timing, and character is \nall over the place among ensemble solutions. The most certain thing \nwe can say is that our weather will remain unsettled and cool.",
